WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.698
An API contract defines expected request
and response behavior.

2
00:00:07.778 --> 00:00:16.545
It should describe required fields,
optional fields, types, formats, ranges,

3
00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:22.122
enums, arrays, nested objects, and
constraints.

4
00:00:22.202 --> 00:00:29.661
OpenAPI specifications, GraphQL schemas,
protobuf definitions,

5
00:00:29.741 --> 00:00:36.415
and written interface documents can all
serve this purpose.

6
00:00:36.495 --> 00:00:42.253
The contract is not just documentation for
clients.

7
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:49.923
It is also a guide for what the server
should accept, reject, and test.

8
00:00:50.003 --> 00:00:58.902
Validation should happen before request
data reaches business logic, queries,

9
00:00:58.982 --> 00:01:05.656
files, downstream services, or
state-changing workflows.

10
00:01:05.736 --> 00:01:12.017
A request body that fits JSON syntax may
still be invalid.

11
00:01:12.097 --> 00:01:14.715
A string may be too long.

12
00:01:14.795 --> 00:01:18.590
A number may exceed allowed range.

13
00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:22.726
An enum may be unknown or deprecated.

14
00:01:22.806 --> 00:01:26.209
A nested array may be too large.

15
00:01:26.289 --> 00:01:35.319
A field may be forbidden for the caller
even though it is valid in another workflow.

16
00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:40.371
Unknown fields deserve a deliberate
policy.

17
00:01:40.451 --> 00:01:49.612
Some APIs reject them to keep clients
honest and prevent accidental over-posting.

18
00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:56.366
Others allow them for compatibility but
ignore them safely.

19
00:01:56.446 --> 00:02:05.868
The danger is accepting unknown fields
without knowing whether downstream mapping,

20
00:02:05.948 --> 00:02:11.313
patch logic, or persistence code might use
them.

21
00:02:11.393 --> 00:02:17.021
If a client sends a field the API does not
recognize,

22
00:02:17.101 --> 00:02:25.999
that should either be rejected or handled
by a clearly documented compatibility

23
00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:26.734
rule.

24
00:02:26.814 --> 00:02:31.787
Safe error responses are part of
validation.

25
00:02:31.867 --> 00:02:39.457
A client should know that a field is
missing, malformed, out of range,

26
00:02:39.537 --> 00:02:41.369
or unsupported.

27
00:02:41.449 --> 00:02:50.347
It should not receive stack traces, SQL
errors, internal object names, secrets,

28
00:02:50.427 --> 00:02:54.353
or detailed implementation paths.

29
00:02:54.433 --> 00:03:02.154
Strong API validation produces predictable
failures: reject early,

30
00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:06.029
explain safely, log responsibly,

31
00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:12.260
and pass only trusted values into the rest
of the system.
